\DOC ENSURES_WHILE_PDOWN_TAC

\TYPE {ENSURES_WHILE_PDOWN_TAC : term -> term -> term -> term -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Replaces an {ensures} goal for a downward-counting loop by a single conjunctive
goal packaging the loop's non-triviality, setup, body, back-edge, and exit
obligations.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes four terms: a loop trip count {t}; two program-point terms
giving the code addresses at the top of the loop body and at the
end-of-iteration test; and an invariant term, a function of the loop counter
and the machine state, whose body in the observed traces is a conjunction of a
state predicate with a flag condition expressing {i = 0}. Applied to an
{ensures} goal, it produces one subgoal with the same assumptions, whose
conclusion is a four-or-five-way conjunction built from the arguments:
{
         A ?- ensures ...
   ==================================================  ENSURES_WHILE_PDOWN_TAC t pc_body pc_test invariant
         A ?- ~(t = 0) /\
              ensures ... (original precondition) (invariant t pc_body) (MAYCHANGE ...) /\
              (!x1. (arithmetic guards relating x1 and t)
                    ==> ensures ... (invariant (x1 + 1) pc_body) (invariant x1 pc_test) (MAYCHANGE ...)) /\
              (!x1. (strengthened guards, including 0 < x1)
                    ==> ensures ... (invariant x1 pc_test) (invariant x1 pc_body) (MAYCHANGE ...)) /\
              ensures ... (invariant 0 pc_test) (original postcondition) (MAYCHANGE ...)
}
\noindent Here the original frame {MAYCHANGE ...} is reused unchanged in every
conjunct, and no assumption is added or discharged.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single-subgoal transformation of an {ensures}
goal.

\ENDDOC
